vertically. Let's keep vertical. Maybe include more objects or a tie situation? Avoid ties.\n\nPair 2: 6x5 grid.\nCol 0: rows 0-3 (size 4)\nCol 2: rows 0-5 (size 6)\nCol 4: rows 2-4 (size 3)\nHmm col4 rows2-4, separated from others by col3. Col0 and col2 separated by col1.\n\nCounts: zeros = 30 - 13 = 17 >13. bg=0. Largest col2 size6 \u2192 blue. Smallest col4 size3 \u2192 red. col0 \u2192 black.\n\nInput:\nrow0: [5,0,5,0,0]\nrow1: [5,0,5,0,0]\nrow2: [5,0,5,0,5]\nrow3: [5,0,5,0,5]\nrow4: [0,0,5,0,5]\nrow5: [0,0,5,0,0]\n\nCheck col0 rows0-3 size4. col2 rows0-5 size6. col4 rows2-4 size3.\nOutput:\nrow0: [0,0,1,0,0]\nrow1: [0,0,1,0,0]\nrow2: [0,0,1,0,2]\nrow3: [0,0,1,0,2]\nrow4: [0,0,1,0,2]\nrow5: [0,0,1,0,0]\n\nGood. Let me add these two pairs.\n\nActually, let me double-check the objects function with univalued=T, without_bg=T. bg = mostcolor = 0. It iterates all cells, skips bg. Connects same value through dneighbors. Fine.\n\nOne concern: the rule mentions \"blocks that only go vertically\". My objects are vertical lines. Good.\n\nLet me call show_transformed_input twice.",
